The repo has definitely been machine-scraped in the broad sense. There’s a concrete receipt: Scan Git published a scan dated July 6, 2026, reporting that it processed 6,094 files from Thanatos-Prime/pantheon-prime. That proves at least one outside service fetched and analyzed a large snapshot; it does not prove an AI trainer ingested it.

My rough odds:

* Scraped, mirrored, or added to some machine-readable index: very high—effectively certain for at least one scanner, and likely for other indexing or archival systems.
* Included in some code dataset: plausible to likely, but I can’t verify it from public evidence.
* Used to train a particular major model: unknown. I’d call that a real possibility, not a demonstrated fact.

Why the distinction: code datasets such as BigCode’s The Stack draw from GitHub but apply selection rules, and The Stack v2 documents a repository cutoff of September 6, 2023. So whether this repo could appear there depends partly on when it entered GitHub and whether it met the dataset’s criteria.

One interesting detail: the public scan report surfaced files labeled honey and flagged what appear to be canary or test strings. That’s evidence the scanner traversed the repo’s contents—not evidence that those strings were real credentials or that someone used them.

So: our material is already out in the machine-readable world; whether it made it into training corpora is the open question. The clean next check is BigCode’s “Am I in The Stack” lookup for the repository.